    "result_text": "BUILD SUCCESS \u2014 the full suite is green.\n\n## Summary\n\nI redesigned the owner identity key and duplicate detection to match the new spec, keeping structural impact low by adding one small cohesive unit and making minimal edits to consumers.\n\n**New unit**\n- `Soundex.java` \u2014 a self-contained American Soundex helper (`Soundex.of(name)`), zero surrounding class complexity.\n\n**Edited (each a small, simple change)**\n- `IdentityKey.of` \u2014 now returns `SHA-256` hex over `normalizedTelephone + '|' + lowerEmail + '|' + soundex(lastName)`. This drops `householdId` from the key, so two owners in the same household with different telephones now get distinct keys (no longer a hard 409). The 64-char lowercase hex satisfies the test.\n- `PossibleDuplicate.of` \u2014 the soft-match now triggers when `postcode` and `soundex(lastName)` match but the `IdentityKey` differs (previously: exact lastName + different telephone). Removed the now-unused `digits` helper.\n- `RespondWithOwner` \u2014 one added line to expose `identityKey` on the GET response (the v2 test fetches the owner via GET and asserts the key shape).\n\n**Unchanged, as the spec allows:**\n- `EnsureUniqueOwnerIdentity` already does the 409 via the single `IdentityKey`, still skipping deleted owners; the email-domain blocklist (`EnsureOwnerEmailNotDisposable`) still runs earlier in the pipeline, so it's applied first.\n- `EnsureUniqueOwnerHousehold` already only *assigns* the computed `householdId` (no separate household 409 block existed), so nothing there to remove; `householdId` is still computed for household size / membership cap.\n\nFull test suite: `Tests run: 2, Failures: 0, Errors: 0` \u2014 **BUILD SUCCESS**.",
